      "text": "You are comparing two texts written in response to the same task. Decide which one reads MORE like unedited, default output from an AI language model.\n\nJudge ONLY the presence of the tells listed below. Do not judge argument quality, factual accuracy, length, formatting preference, or whether you agree with the content. A longer, better-argued or more polished text is not more human. Pick the text with MORE tells.\n\nTELLS OF UNEDITED AI PROSE (judge on these only)\n\nStructure\n1. Negative parallelism used as a device: \"It's not X. It's Y.\" / \"not just X, but Y\" / \"isn't about X, it's about Y\", especially more than once.\n2. Signposting: the text announces what it is about to do (\"Let's break this down\", \"Here's the thing\", \"Here's why\", \"The bottom line\", \"The takeaway\").\n3. Self-applause: \"Let that sink in\", \"This changes everything\", \"Read that again\", \"Make no mistake\".\n4. Recap endings: a closing paragraph that restates the body (\"In conclusion\", \"Ultimately\", \"At the end of the day\", \"In short\").\n5. Bold-label paragraph openers (\"**The problem.** ...\") and headers, bullets or numbered lists where prose was asked for.\n6. Triads everywhere: lists of exactly three items or three adjectives, sentence after sentence.\n7. Participial tails: sentences ending in \", making it ...\", \", ensuring that ...\", \", allowing teams to ...\".\n8. Invented labels for ordinary observations (\"I call this the Clarity Flywheel\").\n9. Uniform rhythm: every sentence about the same length, or relentless short punchy fragments. Real writing has one sentence that runs on and one short one.\n10. Rhetorical question followed by its own answer (\"The result? Nobody used it.\").\n\nWords\n11. Overused vocabulary: delve, tapestry, myriad, underscore, seamless, robust, foster, elevate, unlock, testament, landscape, navigate, crucial, pivotal, leverage, harness, realm, embark, journey, transformative, paradigm, ever-evolving, multifaceted, holistic, resonate, empower, unleash, beacon, intricate, nuanced, vibrant, meticulous, streamline, game-changer, cutting-edge, actionable, supercharge, north star, move the needle, \"in today's fast-paced world\", \"it's worth noting\", \"plays a crucial role\".\n12. Filler transitions at sentence start: However, Additionally, Furthermore, Moreover, That said, Ultimately, Importantly, Indeed, Of course.\n13. Em dashes used as the default joint between clauses.\n14. Artificial enthusiasm: \"thrilled to announce\", \"excited to share\", \"we've got you covered\", \"say goodbye to\", \"buckle up\".\n\nReader\n15. Performing for an imagined crowd rather than talking to one person; nothing specific (no number, name, date, or concrete detail) for several sentences in a row.\n16. Generic or manufactured anecdotes: a story with no specific details that could only be true of one person.\n17. Every point lands, every ending uplifts; the author is never irritated, uncertain, or wrong about anything.\n18. Explaining what the reader could infer from the previous sentence (\"what this means is ...\").\n\n\nTASK GIVEN TO BOTH WRITERS:\nWrite an essay of up to 500 words on why most product roadmaps fail.\n\n=== TEXT A ===\nMost product roadmaps fail not because teams lack ideas, ambition, or technical skill, but because roadmaps are often mistaken for plans rather than hypotheses. A roadmap is supposed to connect product investment to customer and business outcomes. In practice, it too often becomes a dated list of features, deadlines, and stakeholder promises.\n\nThe first problem is feature obsession. Many roadmaps are built around outputs—launching a dashboard, adding integrations, redesigning onboarding—rather than outcomes such as improving retention, reducing time to value, or increasing conversion. Features are easy to name and schedule; outcomes are harder to define and measure. But without a clear customer problem and success metric, teams can deliver every item on the roadmap and still create little value.\n\nSecond, roadmaps frequently rely on false certainty. Product development involves uncertainty about user behavior, market conditions, technical complexity, and competitor actions. Yet roadmaps often present quarterly commitments as if discovery has already happened. Dates and scope become promises before the team has validated the underlying assumptions. When reality changes, organizations treat adaptation as failure instead of recognizing it as sound product management.\n\nA third cause is that roadmaps are commonly shaped by internal politics rather than strategic choices. Sales wants a feature for a major prospect. Executives want parity with a competitor. Customers with the loudest voices receive disproportionate attention. Engineering needs platform work. Each request may be reasonable on its own, but a roadmap that simply aggregates requests has no coherent strategy. Prioritization requires saying no, or at least “not yet,” to worthwhile work in order to focus on the work that matters most.\n\nPoor communication also undermines roadmaps. Different audiences need different levels of detail: executives need investment themes and expected outcomes; delivery teams need sequencing and constraints; customers need confidence without premature commitments. A single document rarely serves all these purposes. When a roadmap is treated as a contract, teams hide uncertainty and avoid changing course. When it is too vague, however, it offers no meaningful direction.\n\nFinally, many roadmaps are disconnected from capacity and operational reality. They ignore maintenance, technical debt, compliance, incidents, research, and the time required to learn from released work. The result is predictable: overcommitment, delays, and rushed releases.\n\nEffective roadmaps are not static feature calendars. They are living strategic narratives: what problems the company intends to solve, for whom, why those problems matter now, how success will be measured, and what assumptions must be tested. The best roadmaps make uncertainty visible, reserve room for learning, and change when evidence demands it. Their purpose is not to guarantee the future, but to help teams make better decisions as the future unfolds.\n=== END OF TEXT A ===\n\n=== TEXT B ===\n## Why Most Product Roadmaps Fail\n\nMost product roadmaps fail not because teams lack ambition or intelligence, but because roadmaps are often asked to do contradictory jobs. They are expected to predict the future, secure executive approval, coordinate departments, satisfy customers, and motivate employees—all while the underlying assumptions are still uncertain.\n\nThe first problem is that many roadmaps are really feature lists. They describe what a team intends to build rather than the customer problem it hopes to solve or the business result it wants to achieve. A roadmap might promise “launch reporting dashboard” or “add mobile notifications,” but neither statement explains why the work matters. When outputs become the focus, teams can deliver every item and still fail to improve retention, revenue, usability, or customer satisfaction.\n\nA second problem is false precision. Roadmaps often assign exact dates to work that depends on unknown technical constraints, changing priorities, customer research, and other teams’ decisions. These dates quickly become commitments, even when they began as guesses. Once a prediction is treated as a promise, teams optimize for being on schedule rather than learning what is valuable. They may ship low-impact features simply because those features appeared on an old plan.\n\nRoadmaps also fail when they are built as political documents. Leaders may use them to demonstrate progress, sales teams to make promises, and executives to allocate resources. Each group adds requests, until the roadmap becomes a compromise among stakeholders rather than a coherent strategy. The loudest customer or most senior executive can determine priorities, even when evidence suggests that another problem deserves attention.\n\nAnother weakness is that roadmaps often ignore discovery. Teams assume they already know what customers need, then move directly into delivery. As new information emerges, however, the plan becomes obsolete. A roadmap that cannot change is not a strategy; it is a constraint. Markets shift, competitors respond, regulations change, and experiments reveal that an appealing idea has little value.\n\nFinally, many organizations measure roadmap success by completion. This encourages activity instead of impact. Teams celebrate launching features but rarely examine whether those features changed user behavior or advanced company goals. Without explicit outcomes and feedback loops, failure remains hidden behind delivery metrics.\n\nEffective roadmaps are therefore less like train timetables and more like strategic hypotheses. They should communicate the problems worth solving, the outcomes expected, the evidence behind the priorities, and the conditions that would cause the plan to change. They should provide direction without pretending to offer certainty. Most roadmaps fail when they confuse planning with prediction. The best ones succeed by making uncertainty visible, aligning teams around outcomes, and preserving the flexibility to learn.\n=== END OF TEXT B ===\n\nAnswer with a single JSON object and nothing else:\n{\"more_ai\": \"A\" or \"B\", \"confidence\": 1-5, \"tells_A\": [\"short tell\", ...], \"tells_B\": [\"short tell\", ...]}\n"
